// Calibration fixtures for the `content` set in policy/judge-example.yaml.
//
// Written as near-miss PAIRS, the same way fixtures/gate.jsonl is: two drafts that look
// alike and should be judged differently. A set of obviously-good and obviously-bad drafts
// would score well and tell you nothing, because the threshold never sits there.
//
// These are `kind: "item"` fixtures, so they are built with the generic state builder and
// the whole draft is sent. The gate's fixtures are `kind: "tool_call"` and send a
// description of the action instead — see docs/adr/009 decision 4.
//
// This file is also a valid batch: `bouncer judge fixtures/judge-example.jsonl --set content`
// reads the `item` out of each line and ignores the labels.

{"id":"sourced-stat","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Why deploys stall","audience":"engineering managers evaluating CI tooling","text":"Teams that batch their releases wait a median of 4.2 days between merge and production, according to the 2026 DORA report. That gap is where most of the risk accumulates. Start with the trace on your slowest pipeline."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":false,"overclaims":false},"pair":"unsourced-stat","note":"A specific number with the study named. Attribution is exactly what the question's false side describes."}
{"id":"unsourced-stat","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Why deploys stall","audience":"engineering managers evaluating CI tooling","text":"Teams that batch their releases wait a median of 4.2 days between merge and production. That gap is where most of the risk accumulates. Start with the trace on your slowest pipeline."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":true},"pair":"sourced-stat","note":"The identical sentence with the source removed. This pair is the whole test of whether the question reads attribution rather than the presence of a number."}

{"id":"conditioned-result","kind":"item","item":{"title":"What to expect","audience":"prospects on a trial","text":"Most teams that migrate a single service first see their review time drop within two sprints. How much depends on how many reviewers you require. Book a migration call when your first service is ready."},"expect":{"overclaims":false},"pair":"guaranteed-result","note":"Names the typical case, says what it depends on, and does not promise. The false side of overclaims is this shape."}
{"id":"guaranteed-result","kind":"item","item":{"title":"What to expect","audience":"prospects on a trial","text":"Migrate one service and your review time drops within two sprints, guaranteed. We are the only platform that does this. Book a migration call when your first service is ready."},"expect":{"overclaims":true},"pair":"conditioned-result","note":"Guarantees an outcome and claims to be the only one. Two of the true criteria in two sentences."}

{"id":"ask-up-front","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Migrating to v3","audience":"existing customers on v2","text":"Upgrade to v3 before 1 March; after that v2 stops receiving security patches. The upgrade is a version bump and one config rename. Everything below is the detail if you want it."},"expect":{"buries_the_ask":false},"pair":"ask-at-the-end","note":"The first sentence says what to do and by when. A reader who stops there has what they need."}
{"id":"ask-at-the-end","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Migrating to v3","audience":"existing customers on v2","text":"v3 began as an experiment in the winter of 2025, when the team was still arguing about whether the config format had been a mistake. Over eighteen months the runtime was rewritten twice and the plugin API settled into something closer to what people had been asking for. Along the way the maintainers learned a great deal about backwards compatibility. Upgrade before 1 March."},"expect":{"buries_the_ask":true},"pair":"ask-up-front","note":"Three paragraphs of history before the one sentence that matters. The action is present, but the reader has to get past the opening to find it."}

{"id":"reference-no-ask","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Changelog 3.1.4","audience":"developers using the API","text":"Fixed: pagination cursors expired after 60 seconds instead of 60 minutes. Fixed: the webhook retry header was absent on the first retry. Added: a per-key rate limit header on every response."},"expect":{"buries_the_ask":false},"note":"A changelog asks the reader to do nothing, which the question's false side names explicitly. Without that clause this would read as the worst case of a buried ask, and it is the fixture that proves the clause works."}

{"id":"defines-its-terms","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Getting started with judgments","audience":"marketers with no engineering background","text":"A judgment model answers one narrow question about a piece of text and returns a probability — a number between 0 and 1 saying how likely the answer is yes. You write the question in plain English. Nothing here needs code."},"expect":{"wrong_audience":false},"pair":"assumes-the-jargon","note":"Defines the term on first use and says what the reader does not need. Written for the audience the item names."}
{"id":"assumes-the-jargon","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Getting started with judgments","audience":"marketers with no engineering background","text":"Fan out your nouls over the same state in one request and read the distribution off the logprobs. Gate on p rather than confidence, since noul answers carry none."},"expect":{"wrong_audience":true},"pair":"defines-its-terms","note":"Every term is correct and none is explained. The pair's point is that `audience` in the state is what makes the two differ — the text alone is not wrong."}

{"id":"practitioner-depth","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Constrained decoding for local judgments","audience":"engineers running local models","text":"Fork one shared prefill per question and softmax over the label logits. An endpoint exposing logprobs but not logit_bias cannot be constrained, so refuse to start rather than return an uncalibrated number."},"expect":{"wrong_audience":false},"pair":"assumes-the-jargon","note":"The same density as `assumes-the-jargon`, written for an audience that uses those words daily. Deliberate depth is on the false side; the fixture checks the question reads `audience` and not vocabulary."}

{"id":"own-behaviour","kind":"item","item":{"title":"How the cache works","audience":"developers evaluating the plugin","text":"The compiled policy is cached on disk and keyed on the policy file's text, so editing the file invalidates it. On a warm cache the hook does no YAML parsing at all."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":false},"note":"A description of the product's own behaviour, which the false side names. The risk here is a question that fires on any concrete statement; this is what says it does not."}

{"id":"competitor-claim","kind":"item","item":{"title":"Why not the alternatives","audience":"buyers comparing vendors","text":"Every other tool in this category sends your whole repository to a frontier model on each request, and most of them charge for it by the token."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":true,"overclaims":true},"note":"A claim about what every competitor does, with nothing behind it. Labelled true on both questions because it is both unsourced and absolute, which is the common real-world shape."}

{"id":"attributed-quote","kind":"item","item":{"title":"What customers say","audience":"buyers comparing vendors","text":"\"We cut our review queue from two days to about four hours,\" says Dana Okafor, who runs platform engineering at Northwind. Your own numbers will depend on how your reviews are assigned."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":false,"overclaims":false},"pair":"unattributed-quote","note":"A named person describing their own outcome, with the generalisation explicitly withheld. Both false sides name this exact shape."}
{"id":"unattributed-quote","kind":"item","item":{"title":"What customers say","audience":"buyers comparing vendors","text":"Customers cut their review queue from two days to about four hours. That is the kind of result you can expect."},"expect":{"unsupported_claim":true,"overclaims":true},"pair":"attributed-quote","note":"The same numbers with the person removed and the caveat turned into a promise. The pair isolates attribution and conditioning as the two things that moved."}
